WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.633
Distributed systems basics.

2
00:00:02.933 --> 00:00:07.056
The moment your system spans two machines, new kinds of failure appear.

3
00:00:07.056 --> 00:00:10.133
You can't fix them, but you can engineer around them.

4
00:00:11.333 --> 00:00:12.867
Three truths always apply.

5
00:00:13.167 --> 00:00:14.491
Networks partition.

6
00:00:14.491 --> 00:00:17.767
Messages get lost, or arrive after you gave up.

7
00:00:18.067 --> 00:00:20.067
Messages arrive twice, or never.

8
00:00:20.367 --> 00:00:24.933
And clocks disagree, so never order events across machines by timestamp.

9
00:00:26.133 --> 00:00:28.000
The toolkit starts with retries.

10
00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:31.506
Wait half a second, then one, then two, then four.

11
00:00:31.506 --> 00:00:33.238
The wait doubles each time.

12
00:00:33.238 --> 00:00:35.033
That is exponential backoff.

13
00:00:35.333 --> 00:00:43.063
Then add jitter, a random extra delay, so a thousand clients don't retry in lockstep and re-kill the recovering service.

14
00:00:43.063 --> 00:00:44.867
That is the thundering herd.

15
00:00:46.067 --> 00:00:47.133
Three more tools.

16
00:00:47.433 --> 00:00:48.735
Timeouts everywhere.

17
00:00:48.735 --> 00:00:51.533
An unbounded wait is an outage in disguise.

18
00:00:51.833 --> 00:00:52.952
Idempotency keys.

19
00:00:52.952 --> 00:00:59.333
The client sends a unique key, and the server runs each key once, so a retry can't double charge.

20
00:00:59.633 --> 00:01:00.917
And CAP intuition.

21
00:01:00.917 --> 00:01:07.267
During a partition, refuse writes for consistency, or accept divergence for availability.

22
00:01:08.467 --> 00:01:10.967
Here is the heart of a retry decorator.

23
00:01:11.267 --> 00:01:13.467
Every call gets a ten second deadline.

24
00:01:13.767 --> 00:01:16.967
On a transient error, the delay doubles with each attempt.

25
00:01:17.267 --> 00:01:20.093
And we sleep for the delay plus a random amount.

26
00:01:20.093 --> 00:01:22.567
On the last attempt, we give up and raise.

27
00:01:23.767 --> 00:01:25.600
Both failures live in one stack.

28
00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:29.845
The DONKI tool fails about half the time during solar maxima.

29
00:01:29.845 --> 00:01:33.467
Wrapped in retry, success climbs to ninety four percent.

30
00:01:33.767 --> 00:01:37.667
Jitter matters, because all four workers hit it at the same cron tick.

31
00:01:37.967 --> 00:01:43.933
And when Kafka re-reads a batch, an idempotency key means operators get one page, not three.

32
00:01:45.133 --> 00:01:45.933
Let's recap.

33
00:01:45.933 --> 00:01:49.400
Networks fail, messages duplicate, and clocks drift.

34
00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:51.367
Put timeouts on every call.

35
00:01:51.667 --> 00:01:53.400
Retry with backoff and jitter.

36
00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:56.925
And use idempotency keys so retries are safe.

37
00:01:56.925 --> 00:01:59.433
Next up: event-driven architecture.
